    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "The case-analysis principle the four blocked laws were missing. Its content is that `Int.subNatNat m n` is `Int.ofNat i` exactly when `m` dominates by `i`, and `Int.negSucc j` exactly when `n` dominates by `j+1` -- and that those two cases are exhaustive, which is `Nat.le_total` turned into explicit differences by `Nat.le_dest`. The `m <= n` half must still split its difference, because a difference of `0` is the NON-borrowing case and has to be routed to the first branch. Stating the second outcome as `negSucc j` rather than `negOfNat k` is deliberate: it makes the two branches disjoint, so a caller learns the sign and not merely the magnitude. `formal.statement` is `Kernel::render_lean` of the admitted type, verbatim from `int_theorem_inventory`.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
